    "q-193605": {
      "dom_id": "step-q193605",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Nancy uses $2\\frac{1}{4}$ cups of flour to make a dozen cupcakes. How many cups of flour does she need to prepare an order for $6$ dozen cupcakes?",
      "worked_solution": "To determine the amount of flour that Nancy needs, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 6.$\n\nFirst, we write  $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{2}=13\\,\\text{R}\\,1=13\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, Nancy needs $13\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups of flour.",
