    "q-60119": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60119",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Fred is $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall. Julie is $\\frac{4}{5}$ of Fred's height. How tall is Julie?",
      "worked_solution": "To find out Julie's height, we need to calculate $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFirst, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($2$) and the second fraction's numerator ($4$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 2}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting this to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n18÷5=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, Julie is $3\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall.",
