      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A country road is $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi}$ long. If $\\frac{3}{4}$ of it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?",
      "worked_solution": "To find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(2\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5\\times 3}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n15÷8=1\\,\\text{R}\\,7=1\\,\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is $1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{mi}$ long.",
