    "q-60605": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60605",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "A box of cornflakes cereal weighs $1\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{oz}.$ Last week, Debby ate $2$ full boxes and another box which was only $\\frac{1}{4}$ full. How many ounces of this cereal did Debby eat in total?",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that Debby had $2+\\frac{1}{4}=2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ boxes of cornflakes. So, to find out the total weight, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 9}{2\\times 8}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{8}=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, Debby ate a total of $3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{oz}$ of cornflakes cereal.",
