    "q-60612": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60612",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "A can of bitter lemon drink contains $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. If Phil drank $1$ whole can and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a second can, how many grams of sugar did he drink?",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that Phil drank a total of $1+\\frac{3}{5}=1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ cans of the beverage. So, to find out how many grams of sugar he drank, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{5}.$    \n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($5$) and the second fraction's denominator ($5$) have a common factor of $5.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5} & =\\frac{5}{5}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Phil drank $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
