    "q-212001": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212001",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A farmer has five $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$-acre fields. He planted wheat in $3$ fields and in $\\frac{1}{2}$ of another field. How many acres of wheat did the farmer plant?",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the farmer planted wheat in $3+\\frac{1}{2}=3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ fields. So, to find out the total acres, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{63}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{8}=7\\,\\text{R}\\,7=7\\,\\frac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, the farmer planted $7\\,\\frac{7}{8}$ acres of wheat.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
